{"kind":"request","text":"Hugh's explicit instruction, 2026-10-09: file a second planner request for \"(2) the counting demo.\"\n\nCONTEST-FIRST DELIVERY\nBuild and demonstrate \"Agents that count\" as the second genuinely different Artroom application alongside the software-development workflow. This REPLACES the proposed small resource-booking example. It does not require completing the larger music/jam application. Prioritize a compelling real, automatically synchronized, browser-based experience that makes the extraordinary flexibility and power of declared applications over events obvious. Competition deadline is October14; reconcile source freeze/recording with current planner commitments.\n\nHUGH'S REQUIRED SCENE\nMultiple independent agents count aloud using TTS, one number at a time in a sequence coordinated BY THE SCOPE. The set of active agents can change while counting. Each completed turn commits/logs the number spoken by the last agent, with its identity. Clients refresh/update THEMSELVES. Web clients are appropriate; mobile phones are unnecessary. Do NOT put an out-of-turn attempt or manual-refresh exercise in the visible demo. Such cases belong in focused verification.\n\nNARRATIVE AND SOURCE REFERENCE\nThe premise is a response to the funny viral three-phone video \"Making 3 AI's Count to100 Together\": https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=MXEInc6Zy7Q . The payoff is that a coordination platform naturally makes counting work.\nThomas Karatzas's browser-based Terse demo is downloaded locally:\n /Users/hughpyle/tmp/Thomas Karatzas - Demo showing how you can coordinate agents with Durable Actors!  This... [2108276055715020800].mp4\nThe original supplied tweet URL was https://x.com/karatzas_thomas/status/2108276198564638886 ; downloaded filename identifies2108276055715020800.\nWe inspected the local recording's visuals and burned-in captions: it opens with the original TikTok premise, switches to browser voice-agent windows, starts counting automatically, brings another participant into the group, pauses/resumes through conversation and disconnects agents while remaining participants continue. Keep our demonstration similarly natural; no protocol lecture or visible failure parade.\nPublic source DOES exist: https://github.com/TerseAI/talkingAgent , inspected master commit0cdf280fbf030808862c2a39606e4aaa8773d414.\n- PRODUCT.md specifies one independent voice agent per web host, shared CountingRoom, start/pause decisions, count+1 cue, increment on playback stop, non-speaking microphones muted to prevent agents responding to each other.\n- src/counting-room.ts persists count/running, selects speaker from sorted currently connected unique participant IDs using count modulo participant count, broadcasts state changes, and handles departure.\n- src/counting-turns.ts drives selected agent audio, correlates its response metadata, increments after output_audio_buffer.stopped, and pauses on human speech.\n- server/counting-socket-relay.mjs and shared/counting-protocol.mjs show realtime connection and typed state boundaries.\nUse as an architectural/product reference; build the application on ARTROOM, not a dependency on Terse's runtime or an unrelated backend.\n\nAPPLICATION CONTRACT\nExpress the counting state, lifecycle, participant changes, guards and completed-turn effects in the shared application definition language/fold. The browser/voice adapter renders and reports progress; it must not be a hidden coordinator that implements all counting rules outside the scope.\nMinimal state: running/paused; active eligible agent roster with deterministic order; last completed number and speaker; exact current turn identity/selected speaker (or equally rigorous derived subject/version); target count (100 for the reference).\nActs approximately Join, Leave, Start, Pause, Reset and Spoken, with any minimal begin/cancel-turn mechanism genuinely needed. Keep definition small, reusable and discoverable to agents.\nSpoken/report-playback-complete checks current turn, eligible designated actor, expected next number and duplicate completion; records number+speaker+turn subject once, then derives the next turn. Record/retain roster changes or the exact roster basis used so replay explains speaker selection.\nActive set changes need explicit semantics at turn boundaries. Joining affects subsequent selection. Leaving the current speaker must either finish its exact current turn or explicitly cancel/pause and issue a new turn; never let a delayed callback advance a replacement turn. Prefer the simplest correct behavior that preserves the natural demo.\nThe recording establishes the agent/client's authenticated playback-completion REPORT; it does not claim proof that every listener acoustically heard the utterance. No exactly-once audible-effect guarantee. Handle late/duplicate responses and interruptions without duplicate journal increments.\nIndependent agents/participants must have real identities and voice sessions. The demo does not need an LLM to invent counting logic; scope-selected number and actor are the invariant. Choose the simplest real voice/TTS integration and adapters compatible with existing hosting/credentials/agent mechanics. Keep credentials private; don't let long-lived model keys reach browser code.\n\nDURABILITY AND RESPONSIVENESS\nRelated infrastructure request: git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3#git:sha1:578edf653772749b5fa9b47f6782f6db4d0ab720 .\nThe counting application can start on the existing cheap DO-backed durable journal; DO commits record completed numbers without waiting for a Git push per number. Use the shared volatile layer for connection presence/attention and other temporary observation where ready, but don't make completion of all possible durability backends a prerequisite.\nPreserve the recorded count through reactivation. Optional Git export/publication is a separate explicit result; definition/source and a selected run can be Artifacts work products. Do not claim an unavailable full archive/export mechanism; use existing verifier/evidence paths honestly.\nClients receive authoritative snapshots/updates automatically (streams/socket or bounded observation) and indicate the currently speaking agent. No manual refresh in the scene. Keep pending/applied/completed distinctions accurate, with small UI latency independent of the much larger TTS/model latency.\nWarm voice connections and prepare allowed TTS work as appropriate; avoid unnecessary per-turn metadata queries, full-history reloads, extra relays and Git operations. Measure application acknowledgment, peer-visible state and audio turn gap separately.\n\nUI / VISIBLE DEMO\nA beautiful, simple stage: clearly distinct agents/voices, current-speaker highlight, shared number and natural join/leave/pause/resume controls. Live updates and count continuity are the foreground. No duplicate headers, giant record panels, setup essay or diagnostic catalog; inspect/replay is secondary.\nSuggested choreography:\n1. Establish three connected voice agents and ask/start counting.\n2. Let the turns make correct sequence obvious.\n3. Add a fourth; it joins the sequence without restarting the count.\n4. Disconnect one, then others; remaining agents naturally continue.\n5. If useful, pause/resume by human voice or a clear control.\n6. Briefly reveal the small application definition and recorded number/speaker sequence, making the same platform's flexibility evident.\nDo not require counting all100 numbers in the final video; show enough uninterrupted turns to establish the behavior within the competition's5-10minute overall demo. Do not commission explanatory failure scenes. The original meme is context; no need to build its three-phone failure.\n\nIMPLEMENTATION / OWNERSHIP\nPlanner assesses dependencies, assigns appropriately skilled implementation/review, and supplies a bounded delivery/recording plan. A separate example application is preferable to counting-specific platform core code; choose exact repository/path/host integration and reviewed targets to minimize contest risk. Any core extensions should be generic declared-acts capabilities, with the application definition owning domain rules.\nReconcile with existing Page/action/notification performance work and shared-language volatile design request8a13f7b84eae5d2656a49352300fccf66696280f. Keep main development journey strong; this second app is contest-critical but should not require a broad UI/framework/storage rewrite or the jam's audio-analysis/timed-music pipeline.\n\nFOCUSED ACCEPTANCE\nReal hosted/platform-backed sequence with at least three identifiable independent agents, distinct TTS voices, automatic client synchronization, correct number/speaker log and real joining/leaving. Verify pause/resume, no-participant behavior, current-speaker departure, stale/duplicate playback callbacks, exact retries/unknown acknowledgment, reconnect/snapshot handoff and restart recovery. Out-of-turn/wrong-number cases verified offstage, not shown in demo. Use real declared guards/fold and appropriate authenticated replay at a stated frontier.\nProvide exact reviewed source, hosted run evidence, timing/turn-gap observations, screenshots or recording with honest source/provider boundaries, repeatable startup instructions and final short scene/script. Focused tests and one coordinated gate per docs/testing.md; no mutation sweeps/competing or repeated whole suites. Report concrete blockers promptly and choose the next bounded contest-ready slice.","body":{"conditions":"Deliver a real Artroom declared-acts counting application and compelling browser/TTS demo: independent agents count in scope-coordinated sequence, active agents can change, completed turns record number/speaker, clients update automatically. No visible out-of-turn test or manual refresh. Provide reviewed application/core slices, hosted evidence, focused verification and recording plan without requiring broad storage or musical-jam implementation.","priority_basis":"Contest-critical second application alongside software development","related_infrastructure":"git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3#git:sha1:578edf653772749b5fa9b47f6782f6db4d0ab720","target_head":"9d7e4c2777ea8d35441b4a9d79b407cd701065fa","target_ref":"refs/heads/main","target_repo":"git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3","to":"20db43a245e9780b92a587425d387d393b17ffc90b89e036ff752399f88fc517"}}